{
 "item": "E13",
 "where": "Proposition 5.18",
 "paper text": [
  {
   "where": "Proposition 5.18 (proof)",
   "text": "Suppose $b,c,d$ lie in three components $D_b,D_c,D_d$. For each $g\\in\\{b,c,d\\}$, Lemma~\\ref{r2:a-cut-single} leaves $D_g=\\{g\\}$, two single vertices in $D_g-g$, or one of the nine forms for $B_g$. Combine these for $g=b,c,d$, with each choice of $\\{s,t\\}$ among $\\{a,u\\},\\{a,v\\},\\{u,v\\}$ and every permitted set of original edges $gs,gt$ other than $ag$. When $D_g=\\{g\\}$, join $g$ to $u$, $v$ or both. In~\\supp{E13}, the combinations are checked using subgraph containment. A DRAT proof certifies that every allowed combination is a subgraph of a recorded toroidal graph or contains a recorded nontoroidal graph. A subgraph of a toroidal graph is toroidal, and minimality makes $G$ equal to any nontoroidal subgraph it contains. Thus $G$ is one of the recorded nontoroidal graphs. The checks leave only $\\mathfrak T_{51},\\mathfrak T_{72}$, and in neither does any $D_g-g$ consist of two single vertices."
  }
 ],
